Here are some things to consider as you architect and build the app:
Building a text-based RPG using Temporal workflows involves modeling each player session or game world as a durable, long-running actor process. Because Temporal preserves execution state automatically across server crashes and restarts, you do not need a separate database or complex state-recovery logic to manage game progress.

Core Architecture Concepts

To map RPG mechanics onto Temporal's durable execution model, break your game down into core primitives:
• Player Sessions as Workflows. Spawn a dedicated workflow instance for each player using their player ID as the workflow ID. This workflow holds the player's inventory, health, stats, and current location directly in memory.
• Player Commands as Signals or Updates. When a user types a command (such as attack, move north, or use potion), send it to the running workflow via a Temporal Signal or Update. Updates are especially useful because they validate the action and return the resulting narrative text synchronously to the player's terminal or UI.
• Inspecting State via Queries. Use Temporal Queries to let the front-end UI fetch read-only information—like character sheets, maps, or quest logs—without altering the game state.
• Side Effects and AI Generation as Activities. Any non-deterministic work, such as calling an LLM to generate dynamic room descriptions, rolling external RNG, or updating a global leaderboard, must run inside a Temporal Activity. If an LLM API times out, Temporal automatically retries the activity without losing the player's turn.
• Long-Running Sessions with Continue-As-New. Because text RPGs can span weeks or thousands of turns, use Temporal's Continue-As-New feature periodically to reset the workflow's event history while passing the current player state forward.

Example Game Loop Flow

1. Initialization. A player starts a new game, triggering a PlayerSessionWorkflow with starting stats and a starting room ID.
2. Awaiting Input. The workflow pauses cleanly using a wait condition (workflow.wait_condition) until a player action arrives, consuming zero compute resources while waiting.
3. Processing a Turn. The player submits "look around". The workflow receives the update, calls a GenerateNarrativeActivity (which might query an LLM or a story engine), updates the player's visited rooms in memory, and returns the text response.
4. In-Game Timers. If a player gets poisoned or starts crafting an item, use workflow.sleep("5 minutes") to trigger delayed status effects or completions reliably, even if the player logs off.
